  "backstory": "Born in 1990 in KOR, KOR. As the sun sets over the rural Korean landscape, the father-to-be, a sturdy man of 31, stands at the edge of his small plot of land, grappling with the weight of expectations. Raised in a household where authority reigned supreme, he embodies a cautious temperament, constantly scanning his surroundings for threats, both real and imagined. His warmth is tempered by an entrenched need for control; while he yearns to be a loving father, the voice in his mind whispers that vulnerability is a weakness he cannot afford. \n\nHis hopes for his unborn child are laced with anxiety\u2014he dreams of a future filled with opportunities, yet fears that the world will not be kind to them. Struggling with the incongruity of wanting to nurture while feeling compelled to impose strict boundaries, he often oscillates between moments of tenderness and bursts of authoritarianism. As he prepares for fatherhood, he clings to the belief that providing material support, however limited, will safeguard his family, oblivious to the emotional warmth that they might actually need to feel truly safe. In the quiet expanse of rural Korea, a 31-year-old mother-to-be navigates the complexities of her own upbringing, shaped by a blend of warmth and authority that reflects her lower-middle socioeconomic status. She often feels the weight of her insecurities, believing that comfort is a luxury she cannot afford; thus, she approaches parenting with an unyielding resolve to prepare her child for a harsh world. While she yearns to provide for her baby, her fears of inadequacy sometimes manifest as an emotional distance, leaving her to grapple with the unspoken message that seeking help is a sign of weakness.\n\nHer home is a tapestry of contradictions\u2014filled with bursts of affection yet shadowed by an undercurrent of distrust in the world around her, which stems from her own upbringing. She carries deep-seated hopes that her child will thrive in a way she never could, yet feels a pang of guilt for the burdens she believes her child must bear. As the baby is born into this world, she stands at a crossroads, striving to balance her instinct to prepare the child for life's challenges while grappling with the desire to allow them the freedom to simply be.\n\nAge 1 (0-3): The world pressed in\u2014safety, warmth uppermost. Small rituals took hold: a way of touching the doorframe, a phrase muttered before sleep. Relationships formed in the cracks\u2014who could be trusted, who could not. Something in the atmosphere lodged; it would surface later, uninvited.\n\nAge 2 (0-3): The world pressed in\u2014safety, warmth uppermost. Small rituals took hold: a way of touching the doorframe, a phrase muttered before sleep. Relationships formed in the cracks\u2014who could be trusted, who could not. Something in the atmosphere lodged; it would surface later, uninvited.\n\nAge 4 (3-7): The world pressed in\u2014safety, warmth uppermost. Small rituals took hold: a way of touching the doorframe, a phrase muttered before sleep. Relationships formed in the cracks\u2014who could be trusted, who could not. Something in the atmosphere lodged; it would surface later, uninvited.\n\nAge 5 (3-7): The world pressed in\u2014safety, warmth uppermost. Small rituals took hold: a way of touching the doorframe, a phrase muttered before sleep. Relationships formed in the cracks\u2014who could be trusted, who could not. Something in the atmosphere lodged; it would surface later, uninvited.\n\nAge 6 (3-7): The world pressed in\u2014safety, warmth uppermost. Small rituals took hold: a way of touching the doorframe, a phrase muttered before sleep. Relationships formed in the cracks\u2014who could be trusted, who could not. Something in the atmosphere lodged; it would surface later, uninvited.\n\nAge 8 (7-11): The world pressed in\u2014belonging, recognition uppermost. Small rituals took hold: a way of touching the doorframe, a phrase muttered before sleep. Rela